course: dictionary
lesson: 9974
title: Teammate: the people on your side
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A teammate is a person who is on the same team as you. Teammate is a noun, so you can say a teammate or teammates. The word tells you who is on your side, in a sport, a game or a school project.
explain: Context gives you clues. «Competing is fine, but hurting a teammate to win is never fine.» You would not hurt someone on the other team, so a teammate must be someone who plays with you. Try the meaning in the sentence: "Hurting someone on your side to win is never fine" still makes sense.
explain: Teammates do not only play sport. «A good teammate» passes the ball, encourages others and plays fair. In school group work, teammates share jobs and listen to each other. When you meet a new word in a story, ask: who is on the same side here?
fact: A teammate is a person on the same team as you, in a sport, a game or a school project.
fact: «A good teammate» passes the ball, encourages others and plays fair.
fact: «Pass to a teammate» means send the ball to someone on your own side.
fact: Someone on the other team is not your teammate, however good they are.
fact: After a loss, people often blame a teammate, the referee or the equipment; scientists call this a self-serving bias.
exercise: What is a teammate? | Someone who plays on the other team | *Someone who is on the same team as you | A referee who blows the whistle | A piece of sports equipment
exercise: «Competing is fine, but hurting a teammate to win is never fine.» What should you never do to a teammate? | *Hurt them just to win | Pass them the ball | Encourage them | Play fair with them
exercise: Which phrase means send the ball to someone on your own side? | Shoot at the goal | Hide the ball from everyone | Hand the whistle to the referee | *Pass to a teammate
test: After a loss, Sam says the shuttle was faulty, not that the other team was better. What is the name for blaming a teammate, the referee or the equipment? | Good sportsmanship | A goal line | *Self-serving bias | Fair play
test: Which is the best way to be a good teammate? | *Encourage them and share the ball | Hide the ball from them | Argue with them about every play | Ignore them when they ask a question
exam: Jo says a teammate is a rival from the other team. Which is correct? | A rival from the other team | A ball used in a game | *A person on the same team as you | A coach who shouts from the side
